# Dev-only reverse proxy in front of the Vite dev server.
#
# Vite's server.allowedHosts rejects requests whose Host header isn't
# localhost/127.0.0.1/etc, so hitting the frontend container through an
# external hostname (e.g. a tunnel domain) gets "Blocked request. This
# host is not allowed." Rather than loosen allowedHosts (which is a CVE
# fix, not a nuisance check), rewrite the Host header to something Vite
# already trusts before forwarding.
#
# :443 uses `tls internal` -- Caddy's own self-signed CA -- instead of
# ACME, since there's no public domain to issue a real cert for in dev.
# Browsers will flag it as untrusted on first visit; that's expected.
#
# The site address is a bare ":443" (no hostname) with `tls internal {
# on_demand }`: rather than pre-issuing one cert for a hostname named
# here upfront, Caddy mints one on the fly for whatever SNI a client
# actually presents at connection time. This is what makes reaching this
# stack over ANY hostname work -- localhost, a raw IP, or a tunnel
# domain someone points at this host -- without hardcoding any one of
# them into this shared, checked-in file. (A named-hostname site address
# doesn't have this problem for its own hostname, but fails the TLS
# handshake outright -- ERR_SSL_PROTOCOL_ERROR in Chrome -- for every
# other SNI, since tls internal without on_demand only knows about
# identifiers it was told about upfront.) on_demand's usual abuse risk
# (anyone can make Caddy mint unlimited certs) isn't a real concern here:
# this is a local/private dev stack, not internet-facing by design.
{
	admin off
	auto_https disable_redirects
}

http://:80 {
	reverse_proxy frontend:5173 {
		header_up Host "localhost:5173"
	}
}

https://:443 {
	tls internal {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy frontend:5173 {
		header_up Host "localhost:5173"
	}
}
